course: dictionary
lesson: 816
title: A word with a picture inside
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are easy once you picture what they are. A belfry is the tower, or the room at the top of a tower, where church bells hang. The words around it give clues: in old stories about churches and castles, the belfry is where bats and owls live and where bell-ringers climb.
explain: Read this: «She heard the bell ring from the belfry at noon.» The bell rings and it is high up, so the belfry must be the bell tower. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Tower fits, so your meaning works.
explain: Some words are used in a special joke. «He has bats in the belfry, always making up silly plans.» Bats in the belfry does not mean real bats. It is an old-fashioned phrase that means someone is a bit crazy or odd. A phrase like this is called an idiom, and its meaning is not the same as its single words.
fact: A belfry is the tower, or the room at the top of a tower, where church bells hang.
fact: In old stories, a belfry is often where bats and owls live and where bell-ringers climb.
fact: Long ago, bells in a belfry told villagers the time and warned them of danger.
fact: «Bats in the belfry» means someone is a bit mad or odd; it is an old-fashioned, jokey phrase.
fact: Belfry is a noun, so its plural is belfries.
exercise: What is a belfry? | A small boat for fishing | A deep cave under a castle | A type of bat | *A tower, or a room at the top of one, where church bells hang
exercise: «The bell-ringers climbed the belfry stairs before the service.» What does belfry mean here? | The bell-ringers' cloaks | *The tower where the bells hang | The church door | The village graveyard
exercise: Jo says, «He's got bats in the belfry!» What does she mean? | *He is a bit crazy or odd | He is very tall | He has real bats living in a tower | He is a bell-ringer
test: «From the belfry, the bell rang out to warn the villagers of the fire.» Why did the bell ring? | To start a music lesson | To call the cows home | *To warn of danger | To announce a holiday
test: Why might an author write «bats in the belfry» in a story? | To say the church roof has a hole | To show that a bell is broken | To describe a real pest problem in a church | *To joke that a character seems a little mad
exam: Which is the plural of belfry? | belfrees | belfri | belfrys | *belfries
